THIRD-PARTY LICENSES — For My Privacy 0.1.0

For My Privacy has no runtime dependencies ("dependencies" is empty in package.json; `npm ls --omit=dev` lists nothing). Its own code is MIT-licensed (see LICENSE). The extension is built with WXT and Vite, and a few very small pieces of those tools are copied into the built extension (.output/chrome-mv3). Those pieces, and nothing else, are listed here.

How this list was made
1. Read the built files in .output/chrome-mv3 (background.js and chunks/countdown-*.js, lock-*.js, options-*.js; the lock and options chunks import the shared countdown chunk) and matched every code fragment that is not from this repository to its source package.
2. Checked each source package's name, version, license field and license text in node_modules.
3. Checked that the build output has no other bundled library code: no import of a package name, no bundler module-wrapper helpers (__commonJS, __esm, __toESM, __defProp), and no node_modules paths.
To check again after a dependency change: build, then search the output for those markers and re-read the minified files. Tools used only to build, test or release (TypeScript, Vitest, Playwright, Node) put no code into the extension and are not listed.

Bundled pieces

1. @wxt-dev/browser 0.3.7 (MIT) — the one-line "browser" handle (globalThis.browser if it has a runtime id, else globalThis.chrome), present in background.js and in the shared chunks/countdown-*.js. Reached through "wxt/browser". Source: https://github.com/wxt-dev/wxt
2. wxt 0.21.5 (MIT) — the defineBackground helper and the small background start-up wrapper with its no-op logger. Reached through "wxt/utils/define-background". Source: https://github.com/wxt-dev/wxt
3. Vite 8.3.8 (MIT) — the "modulepreload polyfill" that Vite puts at the start of the shared chunk used by the lock and options pages (chunks/countdown-*.js). Source: https://github.com/vitejs/vite

